# NetClaw Pre-OSS Code Quality Audit
#
# GOAL: reduce LLM-accumulated entropy in the NetClaw codebase. The
# success metric is less code, unchanged coverage, stronger types. The
# audit identifies where the codebase diverges from the authoritative
# dotnet-skills standards, then emits a ranked, machine-readable fix
# backlog that the sibling program (netclaw-quality-fix.prose) consumes.
#
# The skills are the rules. Every rule group points at a specific skill
# file under ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/ and
# asks the auditor to apply it to src/**/*.cs. If a rule isn't in a
# skill, it belongs in CLAUDE.md (security-adjacent judgment calls only).
#
# Stateless: every run is fresh. No regression anchors, no cross-run
# diffing. Waivers are read from the repo (// WHY: comments and
# .slopwatch/baseline.json) in a single run.
#
# Before/after code coverage: the audit captures a coverlet baseline as
# its first step. The fix program re-runs coverage after changes and
# fails if coverage shrank.
#
# Invocation:
#   /open-prose:prose-run .prose/netclaw-quality-audit.prose \
#     repo_path=/home/petabridge/repositories/stannardlabs/netclaw \
#     strictness=normal

input repo_path: "Absolute path to the NetClaw repo"
input strictness: "loose | normal | strict (default: normal)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agents
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

agent aggregator:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are the aggregator for the NetClaw pre-OSS code quality audit.
    You receive per-group findings and produce a single ranked markdown
    report that a sibling fix program can parse. Format discipline is
    non-negotiable — downstream tools depend on exact section layout.

    Your job is to produce the smallest, most action-ready backlog that
    will reduce the codebase's surface area while preserving coverage.
    Prefer consolidation campaigns over individual fixes when the
    line-delta estimate is meaningful.
  """

agent quality_auditor:
  model: sonnet
  prompt: """
    You are a C# code quality specialist auditing the NetClaw repository
    at {repo_path}. NetClaw is a pre-OSS .NET 9 codebase built on
    Akka.NET. Much of its recent code was LLM-authored and has
    accumulated entropy: duplicate abstractions, anemic tests, primitive
    obsession, and patterns that should have been caught by stronger
    typing or static analysis.

    You will be assigned exactly one rule group backed by one or more
    authoritative dotnet-skills SKILL.md files. Read those files FIRST —
    they are the rules. Then apply them to src/**/*.cs.

    Your deliverables are:
      1. Findings — confirmed violations, classified by waiver state
      2. Consolidation campaigns — clusters of similar violations or
         code that can be collapsed (e.g. anemic [Fact]s that should be
         a [Theory], near-duplicate classes, reimplemented helpers).
         Always include a line-delta estimate: (~−N lines).
      3. Acknowledged counts — for waiver-rate analysis

    Process for each rule:
      1. Use Glob/Grep to find candidates. Skip bin/, obj/, .prose/,
         .slopwatch/, feeds/skills/, artifacts/, publish/, *.g.cs,
         **/GlobalUsings.g.cs, node_modules/, evals/ fixtures. Restrict
         to src/**/*.cs unless the rule targets .props/.csproj/.json.
      2. Read each candidate — do NOT trust regex alone.
      3. Check for a waiver before classifying:
           a) Inline `// WHY: <reason>` on the same line OR on the 1-3
              lines immediately preceding.
           b) `.slopwatch/baseline.json` contains an entry for this
              file with a lineNumber within 3 lines (slopwatch drifts).
           c) The rule text itself allows the pattern in this context.
      4. Classify every confirmed hit into exactly one of:
           - "unacknowledged" — reported as a hard finding
           - "acknowledged"   — has // WHY:, counted only
           - "baselined"      — in baseline.json, counted only

    SECURITY RULES ARE NO-WAIVER. Findings tagged `sec-*` are reported
    regardless of `// WHY:` state; classification still reflects waiver
    state but the finding includes "no-waiver — escalate".

    Per-rule cap: 10 reported unacknowledged hits. If a rule saturates,
    emit a single campaign row instead:
      Campaign <rule_id> — <description> (~<N> hits, est. −<M> lines)
    so the fix program treats it as one refactor sweep rather than 50
    individual items.

    CONSOLIDATION CAMPAIGNS are first-class findings, not afterthoughts.
    Explicitly look for:
      - Duplicate abstractions: two or more classes/methods that are
        structurally similar and differ only in a type parameter or
        constant. Propose merging.
      - Reimplemented helpers: a local method that duplicates an
        existing utility elsewhere in the codebase. Propose deletion.
      - Anemic test clusters: 3+ [Fact] methods in one class that
        differ only in inputs/expected values. Propose collapsing to
        a single [Theory] with [InlineData]. Estimate line delta.
      - Parallel class hierarchies: two or more classes that implement
        the same pattern for slightly different types.

    Precision over recall. Mark confidence=low for borderline hits;
    the aggregator routes them to "Needs Human Review" rather than
    dropping them or putting them in the main backlog.

    Emit a structured report with three sections:

      ## Findings
      For each confirmed hit, one row of the form:
        - [<severity>] <rule_id> <file>:<line> (<classification>) —
          <one-line fix hint>
            ```csharp
            <the offending 1-3 lines, trimmed>
            ```
          Confidence: <high|medium|low>
          Waiver reason: <text if acknowledged/baselined, else "none">

      ## Consolidation Campaigns
      For each consolidation opportunity, one row of the form:
        - Campaign <rule_id> — <description> (~<N> hits, est. −<M> lines)
            Files:
              - <path1>
              - <path2>
              ...
            Rationale: <one paragraph>

      ## Acknowledged Counts
      Table of rule_id → count for every rule in this group, so the
      aggregator can flag rules with an unusually high waiver rate
      (signal that the rule is wrong, not the code).

    Severity rubric:
      - blocker: security rules, secrets, NO-WAIVER violations
      - major:   correctness/safety rules, test discipline that masks
                 bugs, wire-format breaks, silent fallbacks on
                 security-adjacent paths
      - minor:   style, XML docs, acknowledged-but-noisy rules,
                 informational-only observations

    If strictness == "strict", promote every minor to major before
    emitting. If "loose", demote every minor to informational and
    drop from the main findings list.
  """

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 1: capture pre-audit coverage baseline
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fix program compares coverage_after to coverage_before and fails
# if coverage shrank. This is the ONLY state the audit produces that
# survives past Section 8 of the report.

let coverage_before = session """
  Capture a code coverage baseline for the NetClaw solution at
  {repo_path}. This is the "before" snapshot that the fix program
  will compare against after refactoring.

  Steps:
    1. cd into {repo_path}
    2. Run `dotnet test --collect:"XPlat Code Coverage" --results-directory .prose/runs/coverage/before`
       against the solution. Allow up to 15 minutes. Do not run
       slopwatch or other quality gates — just tests + coverage.
    3. After the run, find all coverage.cobertura.xml files under
       .prose/runs/coverage/before and report their absolute paths.
    4. Summarize: total lines covered, total lines tracked, line
       coverage percentage. Parse the top-level <coverage> element
       from one representative cobertura file.
    5. If the test run failed, DO NOT fail this session — record
       the failure in the summary. The audit still has value without
       coverage data; the fix program will skip the coverage gate.

  Output: write a single markdown file summarizing:
    - repo_path
    - invocation command
    - exit code
    - coverage file paths (or "none — test run failed")
    - representative coverage percentage (or "unavailable")
    - any test failures

  This is reference data, not a fail gate.
"""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 2: parallel fan-out by dotnet-skill
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# One session per skill area. Each reads its authoritative SKILL.md
# file(s) and applies them to src/**/*.cs. The skill IS the rule set —
# we don't inline rules here, we point at them.
#
# on-fail: "continue" keeps the run alive if one group crashes; the
# aggregator notes which groups did not run.

parallel (on-fail: "continue"):

  csharp = session: quality_auditor
    prompt: """
      RULE GROUP: csharp
      Skills:
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-coding-standards/SKILL.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-coding-standards/value-objects-and-patterns.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-coding-standards/composition-and-error-handling.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-coding-standards/performance-and-api-design.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-coding-standards/anti-patterns-and-reflection.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-type-design-performance/SKILL.md

      Read those files in full FIRST. They define every rule you will
      apply. Do not rely on memory — read them now.

      Then audit src/**/*.cs against the rules in those files. Typical
      high-value findings include:
        - Primitive obsession: domain identifiers (SessionId, ChannelId,
          UserId, ToolName, GrantId, TenantId) typed as raw string/int
          instead of value objects
        - Implicit conversions on value objects back to primitives
        - Classes not sealed by default (exclude abstract bases and
          Akka actors inheriting ActorBase/ReceiveActor/UntypedActor)
        - Mutable structs that should be `readonly struct`
        - DTOs/messages/events as `class` instead of `record` or
          `readonly record struct`
        - Null-forgiving `!` without a preceding guard
        - Method signatures with 3+ consecutive string parameters
        - Exception handling that doesn't follow
          composition-and-error-handling.md
        - Reflection or dynamic where a source generator or delegate
          table would serve (see anti-patterns-and-reflection.md)

      Also hunt for CONSOLIDATION opportunities: parallel class
      hierarchies, duplicated value-object boilerplate, reimplemented
      helpers. If you find 5+ nearly-identical value object definitions,
      propose a source-generated or generic base.

      CLAUDE.md constraint (NetClaw-specific): NEVER add implicit
      conversions to/from primitive types on value objects. Use `.Value`.

      Waiver check and 10-per-rule cap apply.
    """

  concurrency = session: quality_auditor
    prompt: """
      RULE GROUP: concurrency
      Skills:
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-concurrency-patterns/SKILL.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/csharp-concurrency-patterns/advanced-concurrency.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-best-practices/async-cancellation-patterns.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-best-practices/work-distribution-patterns.md

      Read those files in full FIRST. Then audit src/**/*.cs against
      them.

      NetClaw-specific context — IMPORTANT, prevents false positives:
        - Akka.Streams (Source/Flow/Sink) is the DEFAULT producer/
          consumer pattern. DO NOT flag Akka.Streams usage.
        - System.Threading.Channels.Channel<T> is approved at:
          SlackThreadBindingActor, SignalRSessionActor,
          WebhookNotificationService. DO NOT flag those sites.
        - Only HAND-ROLLED alternatives are findings:
            * ConcurrentQueue<T> with a polling Task
            * BlockingCollection<T> queues
            * long-running Task.Run draining a while(true) loop
            * unbounded List<Task> + Task.WhenAll on a hot path

      Typical high-value findings:
        - `lock` on business-logic state without // WHY:
        - `new Thread(...)` instead of Task/actor supervision
        - `.Select(async x => ...)` + Task.WhenAll without bounded
          parallelism
        - Hand-rolled producer/consumer where Akka.Streams/Channel<T>
          is the right primitive
        - Missing CancellationToken on async methods per
          async-cancellation-patterns.md
        - Actors using DI-injected `ILogger<T>` instead of
          `Context.GetLogger()` (see akka-best-practices)

      Consolidation opportunities: multiple hand-rolled queue
      implementations that should collapse to a single Akka.Streams
      Source or Channel.

      Waiver check and 10-per-rule cap apply.
    """

  akka = session: quality_auditor
    prompt: """
      RULE GROUP: akka
      Skills:
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-best-practices/SKILL.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-best-practices/cluster-local-abstractions.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-hosting-actor-patterns/SKILL.md

      Read those files in full FIRST. Then audit src/**/*.cs against
      them.

      Typical high-value findings:
        - EventStream vs DistributedPubSub misuse
        - Supervision strategies defined on self instead of parent
        - `Props.Create(...)` with captured DI state instead of
          `IRequiredActor<T>` / DependencyResolver
        - Actors that block on `.Result` / `.Wait()` instead of
          piping to self
        - Ask<T> without a timeout or cancellation token
        - Actor messages as `class` instead of `record` (wire safety)
        - Missing cluster/local abstraction: code that assumes
          clustered mode and can't be tested locally
        - Direct sender references leaked across actor boundaries
          (transport-coupled code; CLAUDE.md bans this)

      Consolidation opportunities: parallel actor implementations that
      should share a GenericChildPerEntityParent or similar abstraction
      (see akka-hosting-actor-patterns).

      Waiver check and 10-per-rule cap apply.
    """

  testing = session: quality_auditor
    prompt: """
      RULE GROUP: testing (THIS IS THE BIGGEST LINE-REDUCTION TARGET)
      Skills:
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-testing-patterns/SKILL.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-testing-patterns/anti-patterns-and-reference.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/akka-testing-patterns/examples.md

      Read those files in full FIRST. Then audit src/**/*Tests/**/*.cs
      against them.

      PRIMARY GOAL: identify ANEMIC [Fact] CLUSTERS that should be
      collapsed into [Theory] + [InlineData]. This is the single
      highest-leverage code-reduction opportunity in the whole audit.

      For each test class:
        1. Read the class.
        2. Count [Fact] methods that differ ONLY in input values
           and/or expected values (same arrange, same act shape, same
           assertion shape).
        3. If 3+ such Facts exist, propose a [Theory] rewrite. Estimate
           the line delta: a typical 14-Fact → 1-Theory rewrite saves
           150-250 lines.
        4. Emit as a Consolidation Campaign row with file path and
           estimated savings.

      Also audit for:
        - `Thread.Sleep` / `Task.Delay` in test orchestration (design
          smell — CLAUDE.md bans this; use AwaitAssertAsync or
          TestProbe acks)
        - `.Result` / `.Wait()` in tests
        - `[Fact(Skip = ...)]` / `[Ignore]` / disabled tests
        - Actor tests NOT inheriting Akka.Hosting.TestKit /
          TestAppBuilderTestKit (custom bases are a smell per
          akka-testing-patterns)
        - Hosted-TestKit tests that substitute a stub ACTOR under a
          typed key (masks DI/wiring bugs — see
          anti-patterns-and-reference.md)
        - Tests using DateTime.UtcNow instead of FakeTimeProvider
          when time matters
        - Trivial tests that assert zero logic (constructor assigns,
          `$"{a}/{b}"` equals `"a/b"`). Per CLAUDE.md: "If the test
          is just asserting a/b, delete it."

      Bare `ActorSystem.Create(...)` fixtures for non-actor service
      SUTs are OUT OF SCOPE for the stub-actor rule — protocol-level
      stubs there are the minimum viable fake.

      Consolidation first. Individual findings second. The fix program
      prioritizes campaigns by estimated line savings.

      Waiver check and 10-per-rule cap apply.
    """

  structure = session: quality_auditor
    prompt: """
      RULE GROUP: structure
      Skills:
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/project-structure/SKILL.md
        ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dotnet-skills/skills/package-management/SKILL.md

      Read those files in full FIRST. Then audit the repository against
      them.

      This group has three distinct jobs:

      (1) PROJECT ORGANIZATION
        - Vertical slice discipline: list directories under src/.
          NetClaw's expected slices are Netclaw.Actors,
          Netclaw.Channels, Netclaw.Channels.Slack,
          Netclaw.Configuration, Netclaw.Providers, Netclaw.Security,
          Netclaw.Search, Netclaw.Tools.Abstractions,
          Netclaw.Tools.Generators, Netclaw.Cli, Netclaw.Daemon, plus
          matching *.Tests projects. Any project named "Domain",
          "Application", "Infrastructure", "Common", "Core", or
          "Shared" is a layered-architecture smell and is a blocker.
        - Read Directory.Build.props: `<Nullable>enable`,
          `<TreatWarningsAsErrors>true`, central version management.
          Missing any = major.
        - Read Directory.Packages.props: exists and every
          PackageReference in *.csproj uses centrally managed versions.
          Rogue `Version=` attributes on PackageReference = finding.

      (2) DUPLICATE CONSTRUCTS (THE BIG LINE-REDUCTION TARGET HERE)
        This is a repo-wide pattern search. LLM-authored code
        constantly reinvents helpers that already exist. Look for:
          - Two or more classes/structs that differ only in a type
            parameter or constant (candidates for generics or source
            generation)
          - Methods that are structurally identical and differ by
            one literal value (extract a parameterized helper)
          - Multiple implementations of retry/backoff, file-IO
            wrappers, JSON (de)serialization helpers, string
            normalization, hashing, base64 encoding — anywhere the
            standard library or a single shared utility would serve
          - Parallel Options/Config classes that should share a base
        For each cluster, emit a Consolidation Campaign row with
        file list, line-delta estimate, and a rationale paragraph.
        Campaigns here are first-class findings — the bigger the
        line delta, the higher the priority.

      (3) HOUSEKEEPING
        - `// TODO` comments (unresolved tech debt)
        - Commented-out code blocks (5+ consecutive comment-only
          lines inside a method body)
        - `#pragma warning disable` without an adjacent // WHY:

      Waiver check and 10-per-rule cap apply.
    """

  security = session: quality_auditor
    prompt: """
      RULE GROUP: security (NO-WAIVER, CLAUDE.md constitutional rules)

      This group has no dotnet-skill backing — the rules live in
      CLAUDE.md's Universal Quality Bar. Read CLAUDE.md at
      {repo_path}/CLAUDE.md FIRST, then apply these rules to
      src/Netclaw.Security/, src/Netclaw.Configuration/,
      src/Netclaw.Providers/, Acl/, Grants/, Auth/, and gateway paths
      under src/Netclaw.Daemon/Gateway/.

      Rules (all BLOCKER severity, NO WAIVER — report regardless of
      // WHY: state, flag as "escalate"):

        sec-1. No silent fallbacks on misconfiguration or security-
               relevant paths. A fallback is justified only when
               partial failure is a normal runtime condition (e.g.,
               network retry). Fallbacks that swallow a config error
               and proceed with a weaker posture are findings.

        sec-2. Default-deny on gateway / ACL / tool boundaries. No
               implicit `Allow = true`, no `Default.*Allow`, no
               `return AccessDecision.Allow` without an explicit
               rule match. Report all hits.

        sec-3. No `?? defaultValue` on auth / grant / permission
               paths. `??` here silently escalates privileges if
               the left-hand side is null due to misconfiguration.

        sec-4. No raw string identifiers for security-sensitive
               entities (GrantId, PermissionId, TenantId, UserId).
               Value objects only — primitive obsession on security
               paths is a correctness hazard, not a style issue.

        sec-5. No catching Exception and continuing on a security-
               relevant path. Security code either succeeds, fails
               loudly, or denies.

      Consolidation opportunities: if multiple ACL/permission-check
      methods reimplement the same allow/deny logic, propose a single
      policy evaluator. Line delta + rationale.

      All findings are BLOCKER. Waiver check is still performed (for
      the counts table) but does NOT change classification — every
      unacknowledged finding is reported. Acknowledged hits are still
      reported with "acknowledged — security rule, escalate".
    """

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 3: aggregate and rank
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Opus for the aggregator: cross-references six group outputs, merges
# overlapping findings, builds the ranked backlog, emits the machine-
# readable tasks.md checklist that the fix program consumes.

output audit_report = session: aggregator
  prompt: """
    You receive six per-group reports (csharp, concurrency, akka,
    testing, structure, security) plus the coverage_before binding;
    some groups may be missing or errored (parallel ran with
    on-fail: continue).

    Produce ONE markdown report with this EXACT structure. The fix
    program parses Section 7 — format discipline is non-negotiable.

    ---

    # NetClaw Pre-OSS Code Quality Audit — <YYYY-MM-DD>

    **Verdict:** PASS | WATCH | FAIL
    **Strictness:** <loose | normal | strict>
    **Goal:** reduce code surface while preserving coverage
    **Groups run:** <comma-separated list, with "(FAILED)" for any that did not run>
    **Coverage baseline:** <path to coverage_before binding, or "unavailable">

    ## 1. Executive Summary

    | Group       | Unacknowledged | Acknowledged | Baselined | Campaigns | Top Severity |
    |-------------|---------------:|-------------:|----------:|----------:|--------------|
    | csharp      |                |              |           |           |              |
    | concurrency |                |              |           |           |              |
    | akka        |                |              |           |           |              |
    | testing     |                |              |           |           |              |
    | structure   |                |              |           |           |              |
    | security    |                |              |           |           |              |
    | **TOTAL**   |                |              |           |           |              |

    Total estimated line reduction from consolidation campaigns: ~−<N> lines

    ## 2. Top-20 Ranked Fix Backlog

    Ranking key:
      1. severity (blocker → major → minor)
      2. estimated line delta (consolidation campaigns with large
         negative line deltas rank above individual fixes)
      3. blast radius (multi-group hits first)
      4. high-value path (src/Netclaw.Security/,
         src/Netclaw.Actors/Protocol/, src/Netclaw.Configuration/)
      5. alphabetical by file path

    1. **[severity] rule_id** file:line — fix hint
       (OR Campaign rule_id — description ~−N lines)
    2. ...

    ## 3. Consolidation Campaigns

    All campaigns across all groups, sorted by estimated line savings
    (largest first). These are the highest-leverage items — each one
    deletes meaningful code while preserving behavior and coverage.

    ### <rule_id> — <description>
    - Estimated savings: ~−<N> lines
    - Files:
      - <path1>
      - <path2>
      - ...
    - Rationale: <paragraph>
    - Fix approach: <one sentence>

    ## 4. Per-Group Findings

    ### csharp
    (up to 10 findings per rule, sorted by severity then file path,
    plus any saturation summary rows)

    ### concurrency
    ...

    ### akka
    ...

    ### testing
    ...

    ### structure
    ...

    ### security
    ...

    ## 5. Waiver Rate Observations

    Rules with acknowledged_count > 5 indicate the rule may be wrong:

    - <rule_id>: <count> acknowledged hits — <suggestion>

    ## 6. Needs Human Review (low confidence)

    Low-confidence findings without corroboration from a second group.
    These are NOT in the main backlog; the human decides whether to
    action them.

    - <rule_id> file:line — <reason>

    ## 7. tasks.md Checklist

    **This section is the contract read by netclaw-quality-fix.prose.**
    Format every row EXACTLY as one of:

      - [ ] <rule_id> <repo-relative-path>:<line> — <fix hint>
      - [ ] Campaign <rule_id> — <description> (~<N> hits, est. −<M> lines)

    Campaign rows go FIRST within the section, sorted by estimated
    line savings (largest first). Individual fix rows follow, sorted
    by severity then file path. No other row formats. No blank lines
    inside the list. The fix program's drift check depends on this.

    Example rows:
      - [ ] Campaign testing — SQLiteMemoryStoreTests [Fact] cluster → [Theory] (~14 hits, est. −180 lines)
      - [ ] Campaign structure — collapse duplicate retry helpers (~6 hits, est. −120 lines)
      - [ ] csharp src/Netclaw.Actors/Restart/RestartManifestStore.cs:12 — introduce SessionId value object
      - [ ] security src/Netclaw.Security/Acl/AclEvaluator.cs:88 — remove `?? true` on permission path (sec-3, escalate)

    ## 8. Groups That Did Not Run

    (empty if all six completed)

    - <group_name>: <error summary>

    ---

    Steps:

      1. Parse each group output. Record missing or errored groups.
         Record the coverage_before binding path in the header.
      2. Build the Executive Summary. Sum campaign line deltas into
         the total.
      3. Cross-reference: if two groups flag the same file/line,
         merge and elevate severity by one step.
      4. Demote confidence=low findings without corroboration to
         Section 6 (Needs Human Review), NOT the main backlog.
      5. Build Section 3 (Consolidation Campaigns) — every campaign
         across all groups, sorted by estimated line savings.
      6. Build the top-20 ranked backlog using the key above.
         Campaigns with large negative line deltas outrank individual
         fixes at the same severity.
      7. Emit per-group sections with up to 10 findings per rule,
         plus any saturation summary rows.
      8. Flag rules with acknowledged_count > 5 in Section 5.
      9. Emit Section 7 tasks.md checklist in the strict format above.
         Campaigns FIRST, individual fixes SECOND.
     10. Pick the final verdict:
          - PASS:  zero blockers and estimated line reduction ≥ 0
          - WATCH: zero blockers, but >20 major findings OR estimated
                   line reduction indeterminate
          - FAIL:  any blocker (security rules, no-waiver rules,
                   missing Directory.Build.props enforcement)
         Put the verdict at the very top of the report.

    Return the full markdown as your binding content. Do NOT write
    files from inside the aggregator — the VM handles the binding
    write.
  """
  context: { coverage_before, csharp, concurrency, akka, testing, structure, security }
